// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  THE ASSAY DESK — three foreign results rest on one channel; a `where` guard picks ONE.
//
//  Run me with the RhoCalc interpreter:
//    target/debug/rhocalc demos/flt-assay-desk/desk-accepts-constant.rho
//
//  WHAT RESTS ON @"assay"
//  ──────────────────────
//  Three data, one per contract — each is the NORMAL FORM the Rholang machine computed in
//  `contract-a.rho`, `contract-b.rho`, `contract-c.rho`:
//
//        contract-a.rho   ((K I) K)   ⟶β⟶β   lam x. x            = ⟦λ.0⟧     the IDENTITY  I
//        contract-b.rho   ((C I) C)   ⟶β⟶β   lam a. lam b. b     = ⟦λ.λ.0⟧   the MIRROR    C
//        contract-c.rho   (I K)       ⟶β      lam a. lam b. a     = ⟦λ.λ.1⟧   the CONSTANT  K
//
//  THE DESK'S PREDICATE
//  ────────────────────
//        for( @lambda`${r}` <- @"assay" where lambda`${r}` == lambda`lam a. lam b. a` ) { … }
//
//  Two filters, stacked, and they do different jobs:
//    * the PATTERN `@lambda`${r}`` is itself a Foreign Language Term carrying one typed hole. It
//      matches a reflected guest term by SHAPE and binds the whole λ-term to `r`. This is the
//      EXTRACTION: the foreign result is now a value the RhoCalc program holds.
//    * the `where` clause is the FILTER: it re-quotes the captured term and decides it against
//      the constant combinator K. The decision is made by the substrate, not by the pattern.
//
//  THE SEARCH IS THE POINT
//  ───────────────────────
//  Three data are resting when the guarded receive is offered, and only ONE satisfies the
//  guard. A guarded receive must therefore SEARCH the resting data rather than test the first
//  one and give up. (Until 2026-07-26 it did give up — defect D1, repaired in f1r3node by
//  `6bc58743` / `5d37f67e`. This program is a live demonstration of that repair.)
//
//  WHAT IS LEFT BEHIND
//  ───────────────────
//  ⟦λ.0⟧ and ⟦λ.λ.0⟧ are NOT consumed and NOT destroyed — they are still resting on @"assay"
//  when the program comes to rest. `desk-accepts-identity.rho` is this same program with one
//  changed predicate, and it settles ⟦λ.0⟧ out of the very same set; the integration test
//  (`rholang-runtime/tests/assay_desk_demo.rs`) reads @"assay" back directly and asserts both
//  rejected results are still on it.
//
//  ONE DATUM ON @"OUT", DELIBERATELY
//  ─────────────────────────────────
//  The accept continuation publishes the accepted result and NOTHING ELSE. An earlier draft also
//  published a human-readable label alongside it; two data resting on one channel come back in an
//  order the scheduler decides, and under load the two swapped (caught by
//  `every_demo_file_is_byte_identical_over_consecutive_runs`). One observation per channel cannot
//  be misordered, so the transcript in the run sheet is the transcript every time.
//
//  EXPECT
//  ──────
//        @"OUT"  =  ⟦λ.λ.1⟧                       contract C's result — the constant combinator
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@"assay"!(lambda`lam x. x`) |
@"assay"!(lambda`lam a. lam b. b`) |
@"assay"!(lambda`lam a. lam b. a`) |

for(@lambda`${r}` <- @"assay" where lambda`${r}` == lambda`lam a. lam b. a`) {
  @"OUT"!(lambda`${r}`)
}
